Liturgic-Wave

Ingredients: NEW WAVE + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Woodwinds  
Creative Catalyst: It grew out of a monastery where monks transcribe dreams into modular synth patches.

Liturgic-Wave bridges the angular, twitchy energy of late-70s post-punk with the surreal, hallucinatory drift of heavy psychedelia, anchored by an obsession with organic woodwinds. The sonic landscape pairs mechanical drum machines and chorused basslines with fluttering alto flutes, detuned oboes, and soaring, delay-drenched clarinets. Swirling, phase-shifted textures wash over rigid, motorik rhythms, creating a tension between cold mechanical precision and warm, hallucinogenic breath. Vocal takes range from detached, deadpan New Wave speak-singing to lingering, reverberatory chants that sound like they were recorded in an empty stone cathedral filled with patch cables.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 112–128 BPM. Driving, angular motorik beats mixed with syncopated, skittering rimshots and elastic, un-quantized percussion.
- Harmonic Palette: Phrygian dominant and Dorian modes shifting suddenly into bright, jagged major-seventh chords; detuned microtonal intervals played on woodwinds against steady synth arpeggios.
- Production Signatures: Space-echoed alto flutes routed through analogue chorus pedals; gated-reverb drum tracks overdubbed with tape-warped, frippertronics-style woodwind loops; dynamic pitch-bending on live bass clarinets using pitch-to-MIDI tracking.
- Dynamics: Starts with a sparse, rigid post-punk bassline, steadily building layers of claustrophobic woodwind countermelodies before dissolving into a wash of psychedelic delays, only to snap back instantly to a bone-dry drum track.

Scene & Origin Lore
Liturgic-Wave crystallized in the mid-1980s along the misty coast of Normandy, within the damp stone halls of Saint-Jude-des-Vignes—a deconsecrated 12th-century abbey turned clandestine artistic commune. Resident sound artists, collaborating with renegade Benedictine archivists who used early Buchla and Korg modular systems to map nightly REM cycles onto paper tape, began inviting local post-punk musicians to jam over these generative patch sequences. The resulting subculture, known as Les Decales ("The Unclocked"), embraced a visual aesthetic of tailored black trench coats paired with embroidered monastic scapulars, round wire-rimmed spectacles, and vintage wooden flute cases strapped to leather jackets like holsters. Late-night gigs took place in incense-filled coastal bunkers, where audiences danced in stiff, angular motions while fog machines sprayed lavender-scented vapor over racks of glowing patch bays and silver flutes.

Instruments
- Primary Woodwinds: Alto Flute, Bass Clarinet, Oboe, Vintage Cor Anglais
- Synthesizers & Electronics: Korg MS-20, Buchla 200 Series Modular, Roland RE-201 Space Echo, Electro-Harmonix Small Stone Phaser
- Rhythm & Bass: LinnDrum LM-1, Fender Precision Bass (played with a pick through a stereo chorus)

For Fans Of: Talking Heads, Be-Bop Deluxe, Gong, Tuxedomoon, Stereolab

